BEMUtility-first (Tailwind)CSS ModulesCSS-in-JSNative: @layer + @scope <p>Block, Element, Modifier: a naming convention.</p> <pre><code>.card { }                 /* block */
.card__title { }          /* element of the block */
.card--featured { }       /* modifier */
.card__title--large { }
</code></pre> <p>✅ No tooling, flat specificity, readable. ❌ Relies on discipline; long class names; still global.</p> <p>Small single-purpose classes composed in the HTML.</p> <pre><code>&lt;div class="rounded-xl bg-white p-6 shadow-md md:flex md:gap-6"&gt;
  &lt;h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900"&gt;Title&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre> <p>✅ No naming, no dead CSS (unused utilities are purged), consistent tokens, fast to build with. ❌ Noisy markup; design changes touch many templates unless components are extracted; a learning curve for the class vocabulary.</p> <p>Class names are scoped automatically at build time.</p> <pre><code>/* Card.module.css */
.title { font-weight: 600; }
</code></pre> <pre><code>import s from './Card.module.css';
&lt;h3 className={s.title}&gt;…&lt;/h3&gt;     // becomes class="Card_title__x7a2"
</code></pre> <p>✅ Real scoping, plain CSS, dead code is obvious (delete the component, delete the CSS). ❌ Needs a bundler; theming across modules needs custom properties.</p> <p>Styles written in JavaScript (styled-components, Emotion, vanilla-extract, Panda).</p> <p>✅ Co-located with components, dynamic styles, typed tokens. ❌ Runtime libraries cost performance and don't fit React Server Components well. If you choose this, prefer zero-runtime options (vanilla-extract, Panda, StyleX) that compile to static CSS.</p> <p>Modern CSS gives you most of the benefits natively.</p> <pre><code>@layer reset, tokens, base, components, utilities;

@layer components {
  @scope (.card) {
    :scope { padding: var(--space-4); }
    .title { font-weight: 600; }
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>✅ No build step, predictable cascade, real scoping. ❌ <code>@scope</code> is newer (Baseline 2025); teams need to learn layers.</p> <p>There's no single right answer. Pick based on team size, framework, and whether you ship a design system to other teams.</p>

Tool Enforces Stylelint (<code>stylelint-config-standard</code>, <code>stylelint-order</code>) Syntax, no <code>!important</code>, max specificity, no ID selectors, property order <code>stylelint-declaration-strict-value</code> Colours, spacing and z-index must use tokens Visual regression (Playwright screenshots, Chromatic, Percy) Catches unintended visual changes on every PR Design tokens pipeline (Style Dictionary, Terrazzo) One source → CSS, iOS, Android, Figma Storybook Every component state documented and testable in isolation Bundle analysis + Coverage Dead and duplicated CSS .stylelintrc.json<pre><code>{
  "extends": ["stylelint-config-standard"],
  "rules": {
    "declaration-no-important": true,
    "selector-max-id": 0,
    "selector-max-specificity": "0,3,1",
    "selector-max-compound-selectors": 3,
    "color-named": "never",
    "color-no-hex": [true, { "severity": "warning" }]
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>Senior note · 🔴 Expert</p> <p>The most durable architecture decision is tokens + a small set of layout primitives + accessible component primitives, whatever styling technology sits on top. Technologies change every few years (Sass → CSS-in-JS → utilities → native layers); tokens, primitives and documented decisions survive migrations. Write an ADR (Architecture Decision Record) for the styling approach so future engineers know why, not just what.</p>

